# The cutoff watermark — re-freeze on material, not on the clock (2026-10-07)

Companion to `rolling-cutoff-start-v1`. That memo froze the *value* and its knobs.
This one records *when* to freeze again — the piece the drift-check alert was
missing. "An alarm with no fire brigade": `agent.summary --check` could say the cut
was stale, but nothing re-froze it, and "stale" was measured in hours.

## The problem with time

A frozen cut is still the right number while **nothing new has arrived**. A daily
re-freeze over an unchanged log recomputes the same boundary and produces nothing —
and, worse, a cut that is old *only* because the agent was quiet gets flagged as if
something were wrong. Time is a proxy that cries wolf.

What actually justifies a new freeze is **material**: entries that have been graded
since the last freeze, plus entries that could be graded. The scarce act is the
human grade, so the trigger keys on it.

## The bucket / watermark model

A freeze now records a **watermark**: how many graded entries existed when it was
written (`Cutoff.watermark`). Grades are append-only and latest-wins per
`request_id`, so that count only ever grows and a single integer is a faithful
watermark — no need to store the id set.

```
unused_graded = graded - watermark        # graded since the last freeze
ungraded      = samples - graded          # no verdict yet
actionable    = unused_graded + ungraded  # what the next freeze could work with
```

**Alert when `actionable >= bucket_size`** (`summary.BUCKET_SIZE`, start 20). The
alert is always *satisfiable*: grading the ungraded slab turns it into
`unused_graded`, which then fills the bucket and justifies the freeze. So "the door
is out of sync" always names work a person can actually do.

The bucket is **tunable by how wrong the reviews find Jev** — rarely wrong, extend
it (freeze less often); wrong often, shrink it.

## What acts on the alert

A second Dagu job, `deploy/dagu/gate-refresh.yaml`, runs
`python -m agent.cutoff --if-due` daily, half an hour before the drift check.

`--if-due` is the whole point. An *unconditional* daily freeze would bump the
watermark every day, reset `unused_graded` to zero, and silence the very signal it
exists to answer. So the job asks `summary` first: the **decision** lives in
`summary`, the **action** in `refresh`.

`refreeze_due` is deliberately narrower than the alert:

* **unarmed cutoff** → due once the log holds `DEFAULT_MIN_N` scores (the *first*
  freeze is driven by the log, not the grades — it replaces the provisional 0.91).
* **armed cutoff** → due only when `unused_graded > 0` **and** (bucket full or the
  week-long backstop is overdue). A freeze consumes *grades*; with none unused
  there is nothing to consume, so a full bucket made only of ungraded entries reads
  as **"grading due"**, not "re-freeze due".

Time survives only as `BACKSTOP_HOURS` (a week): it fires when the cut is old *and*
there are unconsumed grades. Time alone never triggers.

## Surfaces

`build_summary` gains `watermark`, `unused_graded`, `actionable`, `bucket_size`,
`scores`, and `refreeze_due`; every consumer (Homepage widget, MCP `status()`,
`/api/summary`, Dagu) reads them from the one payload, unchanged.
